# Starting a task

Before writing code, do these four things in order. Skipping straight to implementation is how work ends up in the wrong stage and gets rejected in review.

## 1. Route the task to a track

The product is already built. **Which track a task belongs to is decided by its size and surface, not by preference.**

| The task is | Track | Read |
|---|---|---|
| A bug, a copy tweak, a new field, one endpoint on an existing screen | **Stage 8 — polish** | `.standards/workflow/stage-8-polish/01-features-to-implement/CREATE-prompt.md` |
| A defect in shipped behaviour | **Stage 8 — flaws** | `.standards/workflow/stage-8-polish/02-flaws-to-fix/CREATE-prompt.md` |
| Removing a shipped feature | **Stage 8 — revert** | `.standards/workflow/stage-8-polish/03-features-to-revert/CREATE-prompt.md` |
| Restructuring frontend architecture | **Stage 8 — arch (FE)** | `.standards/workflow/stage-8-polish/04-architectural-changes-frontend/CREATE-prompt.md` |
| Restructuring backend architecture | **Stage 8 — arch (BE)** | `.standards/workflow/stage-8-polish/05-architectural-changes-backend/CREATE-prompt.md` |
| A whole new module or domain | **Stage 10 — feature** | `.standards/workflow/stage-10-feature/PROMPT.md` |

### The Stage 10 size test

Use Stage 10 **only if all three are true**:

1. **New surface** — it introduces a new business module (`src/modules/<feature>/`), not fields on an existing one.
2. **Iterated UX** — the interface needs several mockup rounds, not a one-shot addition.
3. **New contract** — it adds a meaningful set of new endpoints/entities/events, not a one-line delta.

If any is false it is Stage 8 polish. When genuinely unsure, **ask which track the user wants and record the answer** — do not pick silently.

## 2. Cut the branch

```
task/<short-slug>        Stage 8 work
feature/<name>           Stage 10 work (branch isolation is mandatory there)
```

Branch from current mainline HEAD. Never commit directly to the target branch.

For Stage 10, follow `.standards/workflow/stage-10-feature/01-branch-isolation/CREATE-prompt.md`: snapshot the frozen baseline and define the feature flag **before** any code.

## 3. Write the task brief before coding

Produce a short brief and get agreement on it first. It costs minutes and prevents the two expensive failures: building the wrong thing, and building the right thing in the wrong stage.

```markdown
# <Task title>

**Track:** Stage 8 polish / Stage 10 feature (+ which sub-folder)
**Areas:** frontend | backend | both        ← determines which standards apply
**Branch:** task/<slug>
**Target branch:** main

## Problem
What is wrong or missing today, in one paragraph. Not the solution.

## Scope
- In scope: …
- Out of scope: …          ← be explicit; this is what stops scope creep in review

## Stage ownership
Which stage owns each file this will touch, and confirmation that nothing
crosses another stage's anti-rewrite boundary.

## Contract impact
New/changed operations in `openapi.json`, or "none".
A frontend task needing an operation that does not exist yet is BLOCKED on a
route-back — it does not invent the endpoint.

## Route-backs
RB tickets this task is blocked on, each with an A (resolve upstream) or
B (defer behind an off-by-default flag) decision. "None" is a valid answer.

## Definition of done
The applicable items from the owning stage's `checklist.md`.
```

## 4. Confirm the checklist before declaring done

Open the owning stage's `checklist.md` and walk the applicable items:

- `.standards/workflow/stage-5-frontend/checklist.md`
- `.standards/workflow/stage-4-backend/backend/checklist.md`
- `.standards/workflow/stage-4-backend/sql/checklist.md`
- `.standards/workflow/stage-4-backend/tests/checklist.md`

Every applicable item must pass. An item that genuinely does not apply is marked **N/A with a written justification** — never left silently unaddressed.

**This is the same checklist the automated PR reviewer loads.** Anything you skip here is found there, with the loop cost of a review round trip.

## While working

- **Stay inside the declared scope.** Something worth fixing that is out of scope goes in the PR description or a new task — not into this diff.
- **Do not weaken tests to make them pass.** A failing test is either a real defect or a test that encodes a wrong expectation; both need a deliberate decision, not deletion.
- **Do not disable lint or type checks** to move faster. If a suppression is genuinely correct, it carries a one-line justification comment.
- **Report honestly.** Say plainly what is incomplete, skipped, or uncertain. A brief that overstates completeness costs more than one that admits a gap.
